"Stacks equal-sized dots within numeric bins so the distribution is expressed through countable observations.\n\nUse when: A sample is small enough that individual counts remain useful. Readers benefit from seeing both bin shape and observation quantity.\n\nAvoid when: Large counts require stacks too tall to fit or meaningful weighted observations.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal bin position encodes a numeric interval. The number of stacked equal dots encodes frequency within that interval.\n\nBuild: Choose a bin width appropriate to the sample resolution. Assign observations to bins using a consistent boundary rule. Stack one equal dot per observation and explain any dot multiplier.\n\nCheck: Check dot counts sum to the included sample size. Verify all stacks use identical dot spacing and numeric bin widths. Confirm labels do not imply exact values for binned positions.\n\nPitfalls: Bin choices can shift visible modes just as in a histogram. Do not vary dot sizes while treating each dot as one observation.\n\nExample: Show synthetic household sizes for a classroom sample, stacking one dot per household at each integer size.\n\nAlternatives: Histogram, Beeswarm plot, Isotype chart."
